  {
   "type": "done",
   "answer": "No. According to the lecture, `__syncthreads()` has a scope of **one CUDA block** [S2, p. 63]. It is used to coordinate threads within that single block (for example, to wait until all threads in the block complete a shared-memory load) [S2, p. 63].\n\nThe lecture explicitly distinguishes this from synchronization across the whole grid of threads, which is achieved at the **kernel boundary** by launching separate kernels [S2, p. 63]. Therefore, `__syncthreads()` cannot synchronize threads in different blocks.",
